window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-06-2", "source": "2022年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose you are going to write a proposal to your student union for enriching students’ extracurricular activities. You are to write about what activities to organize and why. You will have 30 minutes to write the proposal. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "A Proposal on Enriching Extracurricular Activities I am writing to propose a few extracurricular activities that\nour student union could organize, in the hope of making our campus life more colorful and rewarding.\nTo begin with, I would suggest holding a monthly book-sharing club. Since many of us read alone and seldom\nexchange thoughts, such a club would offer a regular chance to share insights, broaden our horizons and\nimprove our critical thinking.\nIn addition, I recommend arranging weekend volunteer trips to nearby communities. These activities would not\nonly allow us to apply what we learn in real life, but also cultivate a stronger sense of social responsibility and\nteamwork among students.\nI sincerely hope the student union will take these suggestions into consideration. Your support would mean a\ngreat deal to all of us. Thank you for your time and attention."}, "translation": {"prompt": "从前有个人养了一群羊。一天早上他准备出去放羊，发现少了一只。他仔细一看，看到羊栏 (sheepfold) 上有个窟窿。显然，夜间有狼钻进羊圈叼走了羊。 邻居劝他修羊栏，可是他不听。 第二天，他发现狼又通过窟窿叼走一只羊。他想起邻居的话，就赶快堵上窟窿，把羊栏补好。此后，他的羊再也没有被狼叼走。 故事告诉我们:出了问题及时补救，可以防止蒙受更大损失。", "sample": "Long long ago, there was a man who raised a flock of sheep. One morning, when he was about to take the sheep out to graze, he found that one of them was missing. On a closer look, he saw that there was a hole in the sheepfold. Obviously, during the night a wolf had got into the sheepfold and carried off a sheep. His neighbour advised him to mend the sheepfold, but he would not listen. The next day, he found that another sheep had been carried off by the wolf through the hole. Remembering what his neighbour had said, he quickly blocked up the hole and mended the sheepfold. From then on, his sheep were never carried off by wolves again. The story tells us that fixing problems in time can prevent us from suffering greater losses."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "If you've ever looked at the ingredients list while grocery shopping, chances are you've seen the term \"natural flavors\". But have you taken a  __26__  to consider what these natural flavors actually are?\nMost of us might think that \"natural flavors\" are, well, naturally good for us. A recent study in the journal Appetite found that when the word \"natural\" appears on packaging, people  __27__  that the food within is indeed healthier. In truth, natural flavors do not  __28__  much, at least chemically speaking, from their flavor-boosting  __29__  : artificial flavors. Both can be made in a lab by trained flavorists, but artificial flavors use chemicals to give a product a  __30__  smell or taste.\nNatural flavors come from plant or animal  __31__  , like fruit, vegetable, meat, fish or milk that is then processed or refined in some way. In short, natural flavors are  __32__  from plants and animals to create specific flavors for processed foods. But that does not  __33__  make it easier to tell what is really in your food. Because the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has not  __34__  the term, companies can use it to refer to pretty much anything derived from a plant or animal. And natural flavors can also include a variety of chemical additives, such as preservatives. The FDA doesn't require companies to reveal what additional chemicals a specific item  __35__  .\nSo if you want to know for certain what you're getting with your groceries, you might want to stick to the farmer's market.", "matchIntro": "Fake holiday villa websites prompt warning", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "During the British winter, the thought of two weeks in a coastal villa(别墅) with soul-stirring views of the sea and a huge pool to enjoy is enough to offset(抵消) the labor until the holidays start. For a growing number of people, however, their yearly break is turning into a nightmare as they find that the property they have paid thousands for does not exist and the website through which they booked it has disappeared."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Consumers have been warned to be aware of the potential for deception in this market, which is far from uncommon. In 2017 there were 1,632 cases of reported \"villa fraud(诈骗)\", with victims losing an average of £2,052, according to Action Fraud, the national center for reporting such frauds. \"Millions of pounds are lost each year by defrauded holidaymakers,\" says Sean Tipton of the Association of British Travel Agents (ABTA)."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The problem has ballooned in the last 10 years, with frauds becoming more and more sophisticated. The fake websites have authentic-sounding names involving a mix of keywords, typically including the place name, \"summer\", \"villas\" or \"rentals\". Details of legitimate(合法的) villas are often stolen from other sites. \"When the fraudsters first started it was unsophisticated—the websites looked amateur and there wasn't a lot of effort,\" says Tipton. \"Now they are clever. They extensively rip off legitimate websites and use a different website name. They'll have pictures of a sales team and it might be a poor actor in New York that is down as their head of sales.\""}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fraudsters target popular seaside destinations for British tourists visiting Spain where prices can soar if demand exceeds supply. Prices are kept within reasonable ranges to avoid arousing suspicion. \"A villa might cost £5,000 elsewhere and they will offer it at say £3,500. But a bit of a giveaway is that the villa will be cheaper than on other websites and there's unlimited availability,\" says Tipton. Fraudsters also invest in pay-per-click advertising to feature at the top of search engines when people type in phrases such as \"Spanish seaside villas\"."}, {"label": "E", "text": "With such a degree of professionalism, how can consumers find out if the website they're looking to book with is trustworthy? \"When people book holiday villas they are doing so through rose-colored glasses,\" says Tony Neate, chief executive of Get Safe Online, \"They should be Googling the property, and looking on websites like Google Maps and StreetView to see if it's there. Also, speak to the person you're booking the villa with on a landline phone, as fraudsters tend to only use mobiles.\" He also suggests asking someone not going on the holiday to have a look at the website. \"They might spot problems you don't spot.\" Another potential red flag is being asked to pay by bank transfer. \"The problem is that when the money leaves your account it's in theirs straightaway and it's very hard to track it,\" says Barclays Bank head of digital safety, Jodie Gilbert. \"We generally recommend other forms of payment, like credit card.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Little seems to be known about these fraudsters, \"There is no way to definitely know who they are,\" says Neate. \"It could be anyone. It could be your next-door neighbor or organized crime in Russia.\" Action Fraud says people should ensure the company renting the villa is a member of a recognized trade body such as ABTA."}, {"label": "G", "text": "\"By working with industry partners such as ABTA and Get Safe Online, we are able to issue alerts about the latest threats they should be aware of. If you believe you have fallen victim to fraud or cyber-crime, please report it to Action Fraud,\" it adds. ABTA says it is trying to combat the issue by running public awareness campaigns. \"It's a growing problem and people can't stop fraudsters being dishonest,\" says Tipton. \"They're still going to do it. It's not impossible to stop but as it's internet-based it's harder to pursue.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "Nick Cooper, the founder and co-owner of villa booking company Villa Plus, estimates his company has uncovered more than 200 fake villa websites over the past two years, and doesn't believe enough is being done. \"It is hopeless to report fake villa websites to the internet giants who host them,\" he says. \"I found it impossible to speak to anyone. Also, once one bank account gets reported, they simply use another.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "For now the only way to stop fraudsters appears ultimately to lie in the hands of the consumer. \"When people book their holidays they get so emotionally involved, and when they find that villa at a good price with availability in peak season, they are an easy target,\" says Cooper. \"The public has to learn to be far more aware they are a target for these sort of frauds.\" But it's not just the financial cost. \"A family will turn up at a villa and find out it doesn't exist or the owner doesn't know who you are,\" says Tipton. \"The problem then is you have to find accommodation at short notice. It can be incredibly expensive but it's the emotional cost, too.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "Carla O'Shaughnessy from Sydenham was searching last year for a good deal to book a villa in Majorca for a summer break for the family. \"I was comparing prices online and found one that came in a bit cheaper than others,\" says O'Shaughnessy. She emailed the company via its website, asking how far the villa was from the airport and about local restaurants. \"They came back with believable answers; it was all very friendly and professional,\" she says. Happy with the responses, O'Shaughnessy paid the full amount of £ 3,000 via bank transfer into the travel agent's account and then forgot about it until a month before the booking."}, {"label": "K", "text": "\"I tried logging on to the website and couldn't,\" she recalls. \"I Googled the agent's name and there were lots of complaints about him being a fraudster. If only I'd Googled before but I never thought of it.\" Although she found another villa in time for their holiday, she admits she was much more cautious. \"I paid through a secure third-party site and had phone conversations with the agent. But I wasn't able to relax until we turned up and I had the keys.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Social media can be a powerful communication tool for employees, helping them to collaborate, share ideas and solve problems. Research has shown that 82% of employees think social media can improve work relationships and 60% believe it can support decision-making processes. These beliefs contribute to a majority of workers connecting with colleagues on social media, even during work hours.\nEmployers typically worry that social media is a productivity killer; more than half of U.S. employers reportedly block access to social media at work. In my research with 277 employees of a healthcare organization I found these concerns to be misguided. Social media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it kills employee retention.\nIn the first part of the study I surveyed the employees about why and how they used platforms like Facebook, Twitter, or LinkedIn. Respondents were then asked about their work behaviors, including whether they felt motivated in their jobs and showed initiative at work. I found employees who engage in online social interactions with co-workers through social media blogs tend to be more motivated and come up with innovative ideas. But when employees interact with individuals outside the organization, they are less motivated and show less initiative.\nIn the second part of the study I found 76% of employees using social media for work took an interest in other organizations they found on social media. When I examined how respondents expressed openness to new careers and employers, I found that they engaged in some key activities including researching new organizations and making new work connections.\nThese findings present a dilemma for managers: employees using social media at work are more engaged and more productive, but they are also more likely to leave your company. Managers should implement solutions that neutralize the retention risk caused by social media.\nThey can create social media groups in which employees will be more likely to collaborate and less likely to share withdrawal intentions or discussions about external job opportunities. Managers can also use social media to directly reduce turnover(跳槽) intentions, by recognizing employees' accomplishments and giving visibility to employees' success stories.", "47": "Social media can be a powerful communication tool for employees, helping them to collaborate, share ideas and solve problems. Research has shown that 82% of employees think social media can improve work relationships and 60% believe it can support decision-making processes. These beliefs contribute to a majority of workers connecting with colleagues on social media, even during work hours.\nEmployers typically worry that social media is a productivity killer; more than half of U.S. employers reportedly block access to social media at work. In my research with 277 employees of a healthcare organization I found these concerns to be misguided. Social media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it kills employee retention.\nIn the first part of the study I surveyed the employees about why and how they used platforms like Facebook, Twitter, or LinkedIn. Respondents were then asked about their work behaviors, including whether they felt motivated in their jobs and showed initiative at work. I found employees who engage in online social interactions with co-workers through social media blogs tend to be more motivated and come up with innovative ideas. But when employees interact with individuals outside the organization, they are less motivated and show less initiative.\nIn the second part of the study I found 76% of employees using social media for work took an interest in other organizations they found on social media. When I examined how respondents expressed openness to new careers and employers, I found that they engaged in some key activities including researching new organizations and making new work connections.\nThese findings present a dilemma for managers: employees using social media at work are more engaged and more productive, but they are also more likely to leave your company. Managers should implement solutions that neutralize the retention risk caused by social media.\nThey can create social media groups in which employees will be more likely to collaborate and less likely to share withdrawal intentions or discussions about external job opportunities. Managers can also use social media to directly reduce turnover(跳槽) intentions, by recognizing employees' accomplishments and giving visibility to employees' success stories.", "48": "Social media can be a powerful communication tool for employees, helping them to collaborate, share ideas and solve problems. Research has shown that 82% of employees think social media can improve work relationships and 60% believe it can support decision-making processes. These beliefs contribute to a majority of workers connecting with colleagues on social media, even during work hours.\nEmployers typically worry that social media is a productivity killer; more than half of U.S. employers reportedly block access to social media at work. In my research with 277 employees of a healthcare organization I found these concerns to be misguided. Social media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it kills employee retention.\nIn the first part of the study I surveyed the employees about why and how they used platforms like Facebook, Twitter, or LinkedIn. Respondents were then asked about their work behaviors, including whether they felt motivated in their jobs and showed initiative at work. I found employees who engage in online social interactions with co-workers through social media blogs tend to be more motivated and come up with innovative ideas. But when employees interact with individuals outside the organization, they are less motivated and show less initiative.\nIn the second part of the study I found 76% of employees using social media for work took an interest in other organizations they found on social media. When I examined how respondents expressed openness to new careers and employers, I found that they engaged in some key activities including researching new organizations and making new work connections.\nThese findings present a dilemma for managers: employees using social media at work are more engaged and more productive, but they are also more likely to leave your company. Managers should implement solutions that neutralize the retention risk caused by social media.\nThey can create social media groups in which employees will be more likely to collaborate and less likely to share withdrawal intentions or discussions about external job opportunities. Managers can also use social media to directly reduce turnover(跳槽) intentions, by recognizing employees' accomplishments and giving visibility to employees' success stories.", "49": "Social media can be a powerful communication tool for employees, helping them to collaborate, share ideas and solve problems. Research has shown that 82% of employees think social media can improve work relationships and 60% believe it can support decision-making processes. These beliefs contribute to a majority of workers connecting with colleagues on social media, even during work hours.\nEmployers typically worry that social media is a productivity killer; more than half of U.S. employers reportedly block access to social media at work. In my research with 277 employees of a healthcare organization I found these concerns to be misguided. Social media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it kills employee retention.\nIn the first part of the study I surveyed the employees about why and how they used platforms like Facebook, Twitter, or LinkedIn. Respondents were then asked about their work behaviors, including whether they felt motivated in their jobs and showed initiative at work. I found employees who engage in online social interactions with co-workers through social media blogs tend to be more motivated and come up with innovative ideas. But when employees interact with individuals outside the organization, they are less motivated and show less initiative.\nIn the second part of the study I found 76% of employees using social media for work took an interest in other organizations they found on social media. When I examined how respondents expressed openness to new careers and employers, I found that they engaged in some key activities including researching new organizations and making new work connections.\nThese findings present a dilemma for managers: employees using social media at work are more engaged and more productive, but they are also more likely to leave your company. Managers should implement solutions that neutralize the retention risk caused by social media.\nThey can create social media groups in which employees will be more likely to collaborate and less likely to share withdrawal intentions or discussions about external job opportunities. Managers can also use social media to directly reduce turnover(跳槽) intentions, by recognizing employees' accomplishments and giving visibility to employees' success stories.", "50": "Social media can be a powerful communication tool for employees, helping them to collaborate, share ideas and solve problems. Research has shown that 82% of employees think social media can improve work relationships and 60% believe it can support decision-making processes. These beliefs contribute to a majority of workers connecting with colleagues on social media, even during work hours.\nEmployers typically worry that social media is a productivity killer; more than half of U.S. employers reportedly block access to social media at work. In my research with 277 employees of a healthcare organization I found these concerns to be misguided. Social media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it kills employee retention.\nIn the first part of the study I surveyed the employees about why and how they used platforms like Facebook, Twitter, or LinkedIn. Respondents were then asked about their work behaviors, including whether they felt motivated in their jobs and showed initiative at work. I found employees who engage in online social interactions with co-workers through social media blogs tend to be more motivated and come up with innovative ideas. But when employees interact with individuals outside the organization, they are less motivated and show less initiative.\nIn the second part of the study I found 76% of employees using social media for work took an interest in other organizations they found on social media. When I examined how respondents expressed openness to new careers and employers, I found that they engaged in some key activities including researching new organizations and making new work connections.\nThese findings present a dilemma for managers: employees using social media at work are more engaged and more productive, but they are also more likely to leave your company. Managers should implement solutions that neutralize the retention risk caused by social media.\nThey can create social media groups in which employees will be more likely to collaborate and less likely to share withdrawal intentions or discussions about external job opportunities. Managers can also use social media to directly reduce turnover(跳槽) intentions, by recognizing employees' accomplishments and giving visibility to employees' success stories.", "51": "In the coming era of budget cuts to education, distance learning could become the norm.\nThe temptation for those in charge of education budgets to trade teachers for technology could be so strong that they ignore the disadvantages of distance learning. School facilities are expensive to build and maintain, and teachers are expensive to employ. Online classes do not require buildings and each class can host hundreds of people simultaneously, resulting in greater savings, thus increasing the temptation of distance education for those concerned more about budgets than learning. But moving away from a traditional classroom in which a living, breathing human being teaches and interacts with students daily would be a disaster. Physically attending school has hidden benefits: getting up every morning, interacting with peers, and building relationships with teachers are essential skills to cultivate in young people. Moreover, schools should be more than simple institutions of traditional learning. They are now places that provide meals. They are places where students receive counseling and other support.\nThose policy-makers are often fascinated by the latest technology in education and its potential to \"transform\" education overnight. But online education does not allow a teacher to keep a struggling student after class and offer help. Educational videos may deliver academic content, but they are unable to make eye contact or assess a student's level of engagement. Distance education will never match the personal teaching in a traditional classroom. In their first 18 years of life, American children spend only 9% of their time in school. Yet teachers are expected to prepare them to be responsible citizens, cultivate their social skills, encourage successful time management, and enhance their capacity to flourish in an increasingly harsh labor market. Given these expectations, schools should not become permanently \"remote\".\nThe power of the classroom is rooted in the humanity of the people gathered in the same place, at the same time. Personal teaching is about teachers showing students a higher path, and about young people going through the process together. Technology, no matter how advanced, should simply be a tool of a good teacher.", "52": "In the coming era of budget cuts to education, distance learning could become the norm.\nThe temptation for those in charge of education budgets to trade teachers for technology could be so strong that they ignore the disadvantages of distance learning. School facilities are expensive to build and maintain, and teachers are expensive to employ. Online classes do not require buildings and each class can host hundreds of people simultaneously, resulting in greater savings, thus increasing the temptation of distance education for those concerned more about budgets than learning. But moving away from a traditional classroom in which a living, breathing human being teaches and interacts with students daily would be a disaster. Physically attending school has hidden benefits: getting up every morning, interacting with peers, and building relationships with teachers are essential skills to cultivate in young people. Moreover, schools should be more than simple institutions of traditional learning. They are now places that provide meals. They are places where students receive counseling and other support.\nThose policy-makers are often fascinated by the latest technology in education and its potential to \"transform\" education overnight. But online education does not allow a teacher to keep a struggling student after class and offer help. Educational videos may deliver academic content, but they are unable to make eye contact or assess a student's level of engagement. Distance education will never match the personal teaching in a traditional classroom. In their first 18 years of life, American children spend only 9% of their time in school. Yet teachers are expected to prepare them to be responsible citizens, cultivate their social skills, encourage successful time management, and enhance their capacity to flourish in an increasingly harsh labor market. Given these expectations, schools should not become permanently \"remote\".\nThe power of the classroom is rooted in the humanity of the people gathered in the same place, at the same time. Personal teaching is about teachers showing students a higher path, and about young people going through the process together. Technology, no matter how advanced, should simply be a tool of a good teacher.", "53": "In the coming era of budget cuts to education, distance learning could become the norm.\nThe temptation for those in charge of education budgets to trade teachers for technology could be so strong that they ignore the disadvantages of distance learning. School facilities are expensive to build and maintain, and teachers are expensive to employ. Online classes do not require buildings and each class can host hundreds of people simultaneously, resulting in greater savings, thus increasing the temptation of distance education for those concerned more about budgets than learning. But moving away from a traditional classroom in which a living, breathing human being teaches and interacts with students daily would be a disaster. Physically attending school has hidden benefits: getting up every morning, interacting with peers, and building relationships with teachers are essential skills to cultivate in young people. Moreover, schools should be more than simple institutions of traditional learning. They are now places that provide meals. They are places where students receive counseling and other support.\nThose policy-makers are often fascinated by the latest technology in education and its potential to \"transform\" education overnight. But online education does not allow a teacher to keep a struggling student after class and offer help. Educational videos may deliver academic content, but they are unable to make eye contact or assess a student's level of engagement. Distance education will never match the personal teaching in a traditional classroom. In their first 18 years of life, American children spend only 9% of their time in school. Yet teachers are expected to prepare them to be responsible citizens, cultivate their social skills, encourage successful time management, and enhance their capacity to flourish in an increasingly harsh labor market. Given these expectations, schools should not become permanently \"remote\".\nThe power of the classroom is rooted in the humanity of the people gathered in the same place, at the same time. Personal teaching is about teachers showing students a higher path, and about young people going through the process together. Technology, no matter how advanced, should simply be a tool of a good teacher.", "54": "In the coming era of budget cuts to education, distance learning could become the norm.\nThe temptation for those in charge of education budgets to trade teachers for technology could be so strong that they ignore the disadvantages of distance learning. School facilities are expensive to build and maintain, and teachers are expensive to employ. Online classes do not require buildings and each class can host hundreds of people simultaneously, resulting in greater savings, thus increasing the temptation of distance education for those concerned more about budgets than learning. But moving away from a traditional classroom in which a living, breathing human being teaches and interacts with students daily would be a disaster. Physically attending school has hidden benefits: getting up every morning, interacting with peers, and building relationships with teachers are essential skills to cultivate in young people. Moreover, schools should be more than simple institutions of traditional learning. They are now places that provide meals. They are places where students receive counseling and other support.\nThose policy-makers are often fascinated by the latest technology in education and its potential to \"transform\" education overnight. But online education does not allow a teacher to keep a struggling student after class and offer help. Educational videos may deliver academic content, but they are unable to make eye contact or assess a student's level of engagement. Distance education will never match the personal teaching in a traditional classroom. In their first 18 years of life, American children spend only 9% of their time in school. Yet teachers are expected to prepare them to be responsible citizens, cultivate their social skills, encourage successful time management, and enhance their capacity to flourish in an increasingly harsh labor market. Given these expectations, schools should not become permanently \"remote\".\nThe power of the classroom is rooted in the humanity of the people gathered in the same place, at the same time. Personal teaching is about teachers showing students a higher path, and about young people going through the process together. Technology, no matter how advanced, should simply be a tool of a good teacher.", "55": "In the coming era of budget cuts to education, distance learning could become the norm.\nThe temptation for those in charge of education budgets to trade teachers for technology could be so strong that they ignore the disadvantages of distance learning. School facilities are expensive to build and maintain, and teachers are expensive to employ. Online classes do not require buildings and each class can host hundreds of people simultaneously, resulting in greater savings, thus increasing the temptation of distance education for those concerned more about budgets than learning. But moving away from a traditional classroom in which a living, breathing human being teaches and interacts with students daily would be a disaster. Physically attending school has hidden benefits: getting up every morning, interacting with peers, and building relationships with teachers are essential skills to cultivate in young people. Moreover, schools should be more than simple institutions of traditional learning. They are now places that provide meals. They are places where students receive counseling and other support.\nThose policy-makers are often fascinated by the latest technology in education and its potential to \"transform\" education overnight. But online education does not allow a teacher to keep a struggling student after class and offer help. Educational videos may deliver academic content, but they are unable to make eye contact or assess a student's level of engagement. Distance education will never match the personal teaching in a traditional classroom. In their first 18 years of life, American children spend only 9% of their time in school. Yet teachers are expected to prepare them to be responsible citizens, cultivate their social skills, encourage successful time management, and enhance their capacity to flourish in an increasingly harsh labor market. Given these expectations, schools should not become permanently \"remote\".\nThe power of the classroom is rooted in the humanity of the people gathered in the same place, at the same time. Personal teaching is about teachers showing students a higher path, and about young people going through the process together. Technology, no matter how advanced, should simply be a tool of a good teacher."}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) second\n名词\n【词性槽】动词 take + 不定冠词 a 后，要填名词作宾语\n【依据】take a second = 花点时间，固定搭配\nhave you taken a __(26)__ to consider 意为\"你有没有花点时间去想想\"，take a second\n（second 此处指\"片刻、一会儿\"）是地道搭配，单数可数也吻合 \"a\"。\n【竞争词】B) chance 搭配指向不对\ntake a chance 意为\"冒险、碰运气\"，放进 taken a __(26)__ to consider 成了\"冒险去考虑\"，\n与\"花时间思考\"的语境不符。\nD) counterparts 复数，与 a 冲突\ncounterparts 是复数形式，前面却是单数冠词 a，语法上就过不去；词义\"对应物\"也接不上。\nN) sources 复数且词义不符\nsources 同样是复数，与 a 不配；\"来源\"也无法和 \"to consider\" 构成通顺意思。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) perceive\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 people 后缺谓语，要填动词\n【依据】\"人们认为食物更健康\"是主观感知\npeople __(27)__ that the food within is indeed healthier，承接前句 \"natural\" 带来的错\n觉，这里说人们\"认为/觉得\"里面的食物更健康，perceive（主观认为、感知）正贴这层\"误\n以为\"的语境。\n【竞争词】A) acknowledge \"承认\"含已确认事实，与误解语境反\nacknowledge 是\"承认（某事属实）\"，暗示那是被证实的事实；但本文恰恰要说\"更健康\"只是人\n们的错觉、并非事实，用 acknowledge 逻辑指向反了。\nF) differ 不接 that 从句，搭配不成立\ndiffer 表\"不同\"，固定接 differ from，不能接 that 从句；语法上就接不上 that the food\nwithin is indeed healthier。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— acknowledge 词性对、能接 that，最易误填。它和\nperceive 一样是动词、能接 that 从句，但表\"承认事实\"，而本文讲的是\"人们误以为更健\n康\"。防坑：判断语境是客观事实还是主观感知——讲误解就选 perceive。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) differ\n动词\n【词性槽】助动词 do not 后接动词原形\n【依据】differ from = 与……不同，搭配定死\nnatural flavors do not __(28)__ much, at least chemically speaking, from their flavor-\nboosting __(29)__: artificial flavors，意为\"天然香精与人工香精差别不大\"，differ from 是\n固定搭配，且呼应全文\"两者其实差不多\"的主旨。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) counterparts\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 flavor-boosting 后、their 之后作名词中心词\n【依据】人工香精是天然香精的\"对应物\"\nnatural flavors do not differ much ... from their flavor-boosting __(29)__: artificial\nflavors，把 artificial flavors 称作 natural flavors 的 counterparts（对应物/对等物），正\n是两类香精互为对照的关系，词义最贴。\n【竞争词】N) sources \"来源\"指向错，且后文要用\nsources 意为\"来源\"，但 artificial flavors 是与天然香精\"并列对照\"的另一类，不是它的来源；\n且 sources 在 31 题指\"植物动物来源\"才真正贴切，此处用掉会顶掉后面的空。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— sources 词性也对、义看着也沾边，易抢答。sources 是复\n数名词、配 their，\"来源\"似乎也通；但 artificial flavors 与天然香精是\"对照\"而非\"来源\"关\n系，应选 counterparts，并把 sources 留给 31 题。防坑：前瞻哪个词在别处更对口。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) particular\n形容词\n【词性槽】空在名词 smell/taste 前作定语，要填形容词\n【依据】\"给产品某种特定气味\"逻辑顺\nuse chemicals to give a product a __(30)__ smell or taste，人工香精用化学物质赋予产\n品一种\"特定的\"气味或味道，particular（特定的）正契合\"定制某种风味\"的语境。\n【竞争词】O) strange 感情色彩偏负面，与语境冲突\nstrange 意为\"奇怪的、古怪的\"，带贬义；本句在客观说明香精\"赋予特定风味\"，并无\"气味古\n怪\"之意，色彩对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) sources\n名词\n【词性槽】from plant or animal 后作介词宾语中心词\n【依据】\"来自植物或动物来源\"\nNatural flavors come from plant or animal __(31)__, like fruit, vegetable, meat, fish or\nmilk，sources（来源）指天然香精的\"植物或动物来源\"，后面列举的水果、蔬菜、肉、\n鱼、奶正是这些来源的具体例子。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) extracted\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 are 后接过去分词构成被动\n【依据】\"从动植物中被提取出来\"\nnatural flavors are __(32)__ from plants and animals to create specific flavors for\nprocessed foods，be extracted from（从……中被提取）正是天然香精的制取方式，搭配\n与语义双双吻合。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) necessarily\n副词\n【词性槽】does not 与实义动词 make 之间，要填副词\n【依据】not necessarily = 未必，固定搭配\nBut that does not __(33)__ make it easier to tell what is really in your food，意为\"但这\n未必就让你更容易弄清食物里到底有什么\"，not necessarily（未必、不一定）是地道搭\n配，且与转折 But 及后文\"FDA 没定义\"的语境吻合。\n【竞争词】G) especially \"尤其\"不与 not 构成此意，逻辑不顺\nespecially 意为\"尤其、特别\"，does not especially make 读作\"不特别使……\"，既不地道也接\n不上\"了解成分其实没变容易\"的转折语气。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) defined\n动词\n【词性槽】has not + 空 + the term，要填过去分词\n【依据】FDA 没有\"定义\"这个术语\nBecause the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has not __(34)__ the term,\ncompanies can use it to refer to pretty much anything，正因 FDA 没给\"natural\nflavors\"这个术语下定义，企业才能随便用——define the term（给术语下定义）逻辑严丝\n合缝。\n【竞争词】H) extracted \"提取术语\"搭配讲不通\nextracted 是\"提取、萃取\"，宾语应是物质（如从植物 extracted 出香精），与 the term（术\n语）搭配成\"提取术语\"不成立；且 extracted 该留给 32 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) chance", "C) contains", "D) counterparts", "E) defined", "F) differ", "G) especially", "H) extracted", "I) implies", "J) necessarily", "K) particular", "L) perceive", "M) second", "N) sources", "O) strange"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) contains\n动词\n【词性槽】what 从句里 a specific item 后缺谓语\n【依据】某产品\"含有\"哪些额外化学物\nThe FDA doesn't require companies to reveal what additional chemicals a specific\nitem __(35)__，意为\"FDA 不要求企业披露某个产品含有哪些额外化学物\"，contains（含\n有）正对应\"产品里有什么成分\"，且单三与 a specific item 一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Fraudsters often steal villa-booking information from authentic holiday websites.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】从合法度假网站偷别墅信息\n【定位】Details of legitimate(合法的) villas are often stolen from other sites.\n合法别墅的资料常常被从其他网站盗取。\n【改写】steal villa-booking information from authentic holiday websites ↔\nDetails of legitimate(合法的) villas are often stolen from other sites\nauthentic↔legitimate 同义+动作 steal↔stolen 原样复现\n【辨邻】 H H 谈的是 more than 200 fake villa websites over the past two years 与 once one\nbank account gets reported, they simply use another，主题是举报无门和换账\n户，并未涉及 steal villa-booking information from authentic holiday websites 这\n一动作。\nuncovered more than 200 fake villa websites over the past two years", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Fraudsters keep changing their bank accounts to avoid being tracked.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】换银行账户躲追查\n【定位】Also, once one bank account gets reported, they simply use another.\n而且，一个银行账户一被举报，他们就直接换另一个。\n【改写】keep changing their bank accounts ↔\nonce one bank account gets reported, they simply use another\n句式重构——把'举报一个就用另一个'概括为'不停地换'\nto avoid being tracked ↔\nIt is hopeless to report fake villa websites to the internet giants who host them\n上下文暗示——换账户的目的就是不被追到\n【辨邻】 J J 讲的是 paid the full amount of £ 3,000 via bank transfer into the travel agent's\naccount，焦点在受害者的支付动作，并未涉及 keep changing their bank accounts\n这种骗子方的循环行为。\npaid the full amount of £ 3,000 via bank transfer into the travel agent's account", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is suggested that people not going on the holiday might help detect website frauds.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】没参加旅行的人也帮忙看网站\n【定位】He also suggests asking someone not going on the holiday to have a look at the website.\n他还建议让没有要参加这次旅行的人帮忙看一看那个网站。\n【改写】people not going on the holiday might help detect website frauds ↔\nasking someone not going on the holiday to have a look at the website\n短语原样复现+'帮看网站'与'识破骗局'语义吻合\nhelp detect website frauds ↔ They might spot problems you don't spot\n'帮看出问题'就是'帮识破骗局'", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "More and more British holidaymakers find the seaside villas they booked online actually nonexistent.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】海滨别墅根本不存在\n【定位】For a growing number of people, however, their yearly break is turning into a nightmare as\nthey find that the property they have paid thousands for does not exist and the website\nthrough which they booked it has disappeared.\n然而，对越来越多的人来说，他们一年一度的假期正变成噩梦——他们发现自己花了数千英\n镑订下的房产根本不存在，而当初订房时用的网站也消失了。\n【改写】More and more British holidaymakers ↔ For a growing number of people\n数量增长的同义替换\nthe seaside villas they booked online actually nonexistent ↔\nthe property they have paid thousands for does not exist\nnonexistent ↔ does not exist 形容词与动词短语同义\n【辨邻】 I I 讲的是 A family will turn up at a villa and find out it doesn't exist or the owner\ndoesn't know who you are，描述的是一种典型遭遇场景，并不是 More and more\nBritish holidaymakers find... 那种数量增长的总体性事实陈述。\nA family will turn up at a villa and find out it doesn't exist or the owner doesn't know who you are", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "By checking an agent's name online before booking a villa, holidaymakers can avoid falling into traps.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】订房前 Google 代理人的名字\n【定位】I Googled the agent's name and there were lots of complaints about him being a fraudster. If\nonly I'd Googled before but I never thought of it.\n我 Google 了一下那个代理人的名字，发现有很多投诉说他是骗子。要是订之前就 Google\n一下就好了，可我从来没想到要那么做。\n【改写】checking an agent's name online before booking a villa ↔ I Googled the agent's name\n'Google 代理人姓名'就是'在网上查代理人姓名'\nbefore booking a villa ↔ If only I'd Googled before but I never thought of it\n虚拟语气的反向暗示——'订前没做'='订前该做'\ncan avoid falling into traps ↔ there were lots of complaints about him being a fraudster\n'本可发现一堆投诉'='本可躲开陷阱'\n【辨邻】 E E 的建议是 They should be Googling the property，对象是 the property（别墅本\n身），并未明指 checking an agent's name 中的代理人姓名，焦点与陈述句不匹配。\nThey should be Googling the property", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Fraudsters are difficult to identify, according to an online safety expert.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】在线安全专家说骗子难辨认\n【定位】Little seems to be known about these fraudsters, \"There is no way to definitely know who\nthey are,\" says Neate.\n关于这些骗子，人们似乎所知甚少。Neate 说：'没办法真正确定他们是谁。'\n【改写】Fraudsters are difficult to identify ↔ Little seems to be known about these fraudsters\n'知之甚少'就是'难以辨认'\nFraudsters are difficult to identify ↔ There is no way to definitely know who they are\n'没办法确定他们是谁'='难以辨认'\naccording to an online safety expert ↔ says Neate\nNeate=Get Safe Online 的 CEO=在线安全专家\n【辨邻】 E E 中 Neate 说的是 They should be Googling the property, and looking on\nwebsites like Google Maps and StreetView to see if it's there，焦点是具体防骗动\n作，并未触及 Fraudsters are difficult to identify 这个判断。\nchief executive of Get Safe Online", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Holidaymakers have been alerted to the frequent occurrence of online villa-booking frauds.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】提醒度假者警惕骗局频发\n【定位】Consumers have been warned to be aware of the potential for deception in this market, which\nis far from uncommon.\n消费者已经被警告要警惕这一市场中可能存在的欺诈行为，这种欺诈绝非少见。\n【改写】Holidaymakers have been alerted ↔ Consumers have been warned\nalerted ↔ warned 同义+被动结构同形\nto the frequent occurrence of online villa-booking frauds ↔ which is far from uncommon\n'far from uncommon' 双重否定='frequent occurrence'\n【辨邻】 G G 说 we are able to issue alerts about the latest threats they should be aware of\n与 running public awareness campaigns，焦点是机构层面的行动，并未像\nConsumers have been warned 那样把'消费者被警告'作为主句陈述出来。\nwe are able to issue alerts about the latest threats they should be aware of", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "It is holidaymakers that can protect themselves from falling victim to frauds.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】防骗最终靠度假者自己\n【定位】For now the only way to stop fraudsters appears ultimately to lie in the hands of\nthe consumer.\n就目前而言，阻止骗子的唯一办法似乎最终落在消费者自己手中。\n【改写】It is holidaymakers that can protect themselves from falling victim to frauds ↔\nthe only way to stop fraudsters appears ultimately to lie in the hands of the consumer\n'最终落在消费者手中' = '度假者自己保护自己'\nfrom falling victim to frauds ↔\nThe public has to learn to be far more aware they are a target for these sort of frauds\n'公众需提高警觉避免成为骗局目标'='避免上当受骗'\n【辨邻】 E E 列举了 They should be Googling the property 等具体动作，是'操作层'的防骗方\n法，并未做出 It is holidaymakers that... 这种'最终责任归属'的判断。\nThey should be Googling the property, and looking on websites like Google Maps and StreetView\nto see if it's there", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Holidaymakers are advised not to make payments by bank transfer.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不建议用银行转账付款\n【定位】Another potential red flag is being asked to pay by bank transfer.\n另一个可能的危险信号，是对方要求你用银行转账付款。\n【改写】Holidaymakers are advised not to make payments by bank transfer ↔\nAnother potential red flag is being asked to pay by bank transfer\n'红旗'='危险信号'='不建议'\nHolidaymakers are advised not to make payments by bank transfer ↔\nWe generally recommend other forms of payment, like credit card\n'建议其他支付方式'='建议不要银行转账'\n【辨邻】 J J 中 paid the full amount of £ 3,000 via bank transfer 只是描述受害者 Carla 的实\n际付款行为，并未给出 advised not to 这一警示性建议。\npaid the full amount of £ 3,000 via bank transfer into the travel agent's account", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Fraudsters advertise their villas at reasonable prices so as not to be suspected.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】定价合理以免被怀疑\n【定位】Prices are kept within reasonable ranges to avoid arousing suspicion.\n价格被控制在合理范围内，以避免引起怀疑。\n【改写】advertise their villas at reasonable prices ↔ Prices are kept within reasonable ranges\n'合理价格'同词复现+'打广告'与'定价'语义吻合\nso as not to be suspected ↔ to avoid arousing suspicion\n目的状语句式转换\n【辨邻】 C C 谈的是 When the fraudsters first started it was unsophisticated 与 They\nextensively rip off legitimate websites，重点是手法升级与盗资料，并未陈述\nreasonable prices 这一定价策略。\nNow they are clever. They extensively rip off legitimate websites and use a different website name", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does previous research about social media reveal?", "options": ["A) Most employees think positively of it.", "B) It improves employees' work efficiency.", "C) It enables employees to form connections.", "D) Employees spend much of their work time on it."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does previous research about social media reveal? 用 what...reveal 直接问研究\n揭示的事实，没有 infer/imply 等推断信号，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does previous research about social media reveal? 结构平铺：what 作\nreveal 的宾语前置，about social media 是修饰 research 的介词短语。无陷阱。\n【定位】Research has shown that 82% of employees think social media can improve work\nrelationships and 60% believe it can support decision-making processes.\n已有研究表明：82% 的员工认为社交媒体能改善工作关系，60% 的员工相信它有助于决策\n过程。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\n82% of employees think social media can improve work relationships and 60% believe it can\nsupport decision-making processes\n↔ Most employees think positively of it\n原文两条数据都是正面态度的多数比例，恰好对应预判中的\"多数员工肯定它\"，整段不\n含工作效率提升或工作时间被占用的客观数据，故锁定选项首字母为 A。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文只说员工\"认为\"社交媒体能改善关系和决策，是主观看法；It improves\nemployees' work efficiency 把这种看法说成了已被证明的客观事实，且把 work\nrelationships 偷换成 efficiency。\nC) 以偏概全\nIt enables employees to form connections 把原文 improve work relationships 改\n成\"建立联系\"，并把\"员工的看法\"偷换成\"它实际能够\"，只触及关系一项却丢了决策，且\n把主观信念伪装成已证事实。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只说员工 connecting with colleagues on social media, even during work\nhours，并没有给出\"花费大量工作时间在上面\"的程度判断；Employees spend much\nof their work time on it 凭空加上了\"花费大量时间\"的数量评估。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did the author's own research find about social media?", "options": ["A) It influences employees' work negatively.", "B) It does much harm to employee loyalty.", "C) It kills employees' motivation for work", "D) It affects employers' decision-making."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What did the author's own research find about social media? 用 what...find 问研究的\n客观发现，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What did the author's own research find about social media? 主语 the author's\nown research，谓语 find，about social media 是 find 后的话题状语。结构清楚，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Social media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it kills employee retention.\n社交媒体降低生产力的程度，远远比不上它对员工留任的杀伤力。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文 Social media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it kills employee\nretention 已点明社交媒体并不显著降低生产力，所以\"对工作产生负面影响\"过于宽泛\n——它只在留任这一面有显著负面，工作本身（生产力、动力）并没有受到普遍负面\n影响。\nB) ✓ 正确\nSocial media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it kills employee retention ↔\nIt does much harm to employee loyalty\nnot...as much as... 句式的语义重心在后半句\"as much as\"之后，定位句的论点核心\n是\"对员工留任的杀伤更大\"，恰好对应预判与首字母 B 的选项。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文第 2 段并未谈及对员工动力（motivation）的杀伤——动力一词出现在第 3 段，且\n第 3 段中社交媒体反而是\"使员工 more motivated\"。本选项把作者研究的核心结论说\n成\"扼杀动力\"，与原文位置和方向都不符。\nD) 答非所问\n原文第 1 段提及社交媒体被员工认为 can support decision-making processes，主语\n是\"员工的决策\"，而非\"雇主的决策\"；本选项把\"对雇主决策的影响\"放进作者己研结论\n里，既错位也答非所问。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What did the author find in his study about the effect of online social interactions?", "options": ["A) It differs from employee to employee.", "B) It tends to vary with the platform used.", "C) It has much to do with whom employees interact with.", "D) It is hard to measure when employees interact with outsiders."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What did the author find in his study about the effect of online social interactions?\n同样是 what...find 句式，问研究中的具体发现。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What did the author find，in his study 是状语，about the effect of online\nsocial interactions 是 find 的宾语介词短语，进一步限定要找的发现内容。结构略长，但层\n层并列，无陷阱。\n【定位】I found employees who engage in online social interactions with co-workers through social\nmedia blogs tend to be more motivated and come up with innovative ideas. But when\nemployees interact with individuals outside the organization, they are less motivated and\nshow less initiative.\n我发现：通过社交媒体博客与同事进行在线社交互动的员工，往往更有动力、能想出更多新\n点子；但当员工与组织外的人互动时，他们反而动力下降、缺少主动性。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文并未说\"因人而异\"或\"每个员工不一样\"，而是把员工按互动对象分成两类得出两种结\n果；It differs from employee to employee 把分类标准偷换成\"员工个体差异\"。\nB) 无中生有\n原文 platforms like Facebook, Twitter, or LinkedIn 仅作为研究背景，没有把不同平台\n作为造成结果差异的原因；It tends to vary with the platform used 把背景信息硬升级\n成变量来源。\nC) ✓ 正确\nemployees who engage in online social interactions with co-workers ... tend to be more\nmotivated / when employees interact with individuals outside the organization, they are less\nmotivated\n↔ It has much to do with whom employees interact with\n原文两个分句除了对象不同（同事 vs. 外部人士）几乎所有别的条件都相同，结果却完\n全相反——这正是\"效果取决于互动对象\"的最直接证据，故锁定首字母 C。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰已经测量出员工与外部人士互动会导致 less motivated and show less\ninitiative，研究是给出了明确结果的，并不\"难以衡量\"。本选项与原文方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What problem was found with employees using social media for work?", "options": ["A) They seldom expressed their inner thoughts.", "B) Most of them explored new job opportunities.", "C) They were reluctant to collaborate with others.", "D) Many of them ended with lower productivity."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What problem was found with employees using social media for work? 用\nwhat...found 问研究中发现的问题，仍是问客观事实。\n【拆句】题干 What problem was found with employees using social media for work? 是 what\n作主语的被动疑问句：problem 与 what 共同作主语，be found 是谓语，with\nemployees using social media for work 是介词短语修饰 problem。读不顺时把它倒过来\n想成 \"researchers found what problem with these employees\" 即可。\n【定位】I found 76% of employees using social media for work took an interest in other organizations\nthey found on social media. When I examined how respondents expressed openness to new\ncareers and employers, I found that they engaged in some key activities including researching\nnew organizations and making new work connections.\n我发现：76% 用社交媒体办公的员工会关注他们在社交媒体上看到的其他组织。当我考察\n这些受访者如何表达对新职业、新雇主的开放态度时，我发现他们会从事几项关键活动，包\n括研究新公司、建立新的工作联系。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文第 4 段没有提到员工\"是否表达内心想法\"——只说他们关注其他组织、研究新公\n司、建立新人脉，这些都是外向行为；They seldom expressed their inner thoughts\n把不存在的话题拉进来。\nB) ✓ 正确\n76% of employees using social media for work took an interest in other organizations they found\non social media\n↔ Most of them explored new job opportunities\nopenness to new careers and employers ↔ explored new job opportunities\n76% 的覆盖面+关注新公司、研究新公司、建立新人脉、对新职业开放等多项行为同向\n叠加，正对应\"多数人在物色新工作\"，故锁定首字母 B。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文第 3 段已说与同事互动会让员工 more motivated and come up with innovative\nideas，本身就是合作意愿强；They were reluctant to collaborate with others 与之\n方向相反。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文第 2 段已经否定 Social media doesn't reduce productivity nearly as much as it\nkills employee retention，社交媒体并未明显降低生产力；本选项却说\"很多人最终生\n产力降低\"，是把作者明确否定的结论又重新放回来。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author suggest managers do to neutralize the retention risk?", "options": ["A) Give promotions to employees for their accomplishments.", "B) Create opportunities for employees to share success stories.", "C) Acknowledge employees' achievements through social media.", "D) Encourage employees to increase their visibility on social media."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest managers do to neutralize the retention risk? 虽含\nsuggest，但问的是作者明文给出的建议方案，原文必有对应句，仍属细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author suggest managers do，managers do 是 suggest 后\n省略 that 的虚拟语气从句（suggest sb. do），to neutralize the retention risk 是表目的\n的不定式。难点在虚拟语气省略 that 的结构，主体考生易把 managers 看成全句主语。\n【定位】Managers can also use social media to directly reduce turnover(跳槽) intentions, by\nrecognizing employees' accomplishments and giving visibility to employees' success stories.\n管理者还可以利用社交媒体直接降低跳槽意向：方法是表彰员工取得的成就、让员工的成功\n故事更被看见。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文动作是 recognizing employees' accomplishments（公开承认成就），不是\"提\n拔\"；Give promotions to employees for their accomplishments 把\"承认\"偷换成\"提\n拔\"，并丢掉了\"通过社交媒体\"这一关键媒介。\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文是管理者借社交媒体 giving visibility to employees' success stories——动作发\n起者是管理者；Create opportunities for employees to share success stories 把动作\n主体换成员工自己分享，与原文恰好对调，且回避了\"用社交媒体\"这一关键媒介。\nC) ✓ 正确\nManagers can also use social media to directly reduce turnover(跳槽) intentions, by recognizing\nemployees' accomplishments and giving visibility to employees' success stories\n↔ Acknowledge employees' achievements through social media\n预判要求\"用社交媒体表彰成就\"中的动作与媒介两要素同时对齐，只有首字母 C 把\"承认\n成就\"与\"借助社交媒体\"都准确保留，其余选项要么换错动作、要么丢掉媒介。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是管理者 giving visibility to employees' success stories——是\"让员工的事迹可\n见\"，主导者是管理者；Encourage employees to increase their visibility on social\nmedia 把\"被表彰的事迹\"偷换成\"员工的曝光度\"，且把管理者的动作变成鼓励员工自己\n增加曝光，正好是文章想抑制的\"个人外向行为\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What mainly accounts for the possibility that distance learning could become the norm?", "options": ["A) Advances in education technology.", "B) Shrinking financial resources.", "C) Shortage of school facilities.", "D) Lack of qualified teachers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What mainly accounts for the possibility that distance learning could become the\nnorm?问「是什么导致」，是对文本中明确陈述事实的提取，不是态度也不是推断。\n【拆句】题干主干是What mainly accounts for the possibility，that引导同位语从句解释\npossibility的内容；that distance learning could become the norm整体作possibility的\n同位语；副词mainly限定account for，提示要找「主要」原因而非次要原因。结构清晰、\n无陷阱。\n【定位】In the coming era of budget cuts to education, distance learning could become the norm.\n在即将到来的教育预算削减时代，远程教育可能成为常态。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文未提及教育技术本身的进步是导致远程教育常态化的原因；P1只字未提技术进步，\n只谈预算。\nB) ✓ 正确\nbudget cuts to education ↔ Shrinking financial resources\n预判「教育预算削减」与Shrinking financial resources「财政资源缩水」是同一概念的\n不同表述，是细节题最典型的同义替换。\nC) 偷换概念\nShortage of school facilities是「校舍短缺」，而原文School facilities are expensive\nto build and maintain说的是校舍「造价高、维护贵」，是「贵」不是「缺」；且这是\n第二段提到的，并非第一段中远程教育常态化的原因。\nD) 无中生有\n原文从未提及「合格教师短缺」是远程教育常态化的原因；teachers are expensive to\nemploy说的是教师「贵」，不是「缺」或「不合格」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author say is one possible benefit of students attending school physically?", "options": ["A) Developing the habit of getting up early.", "B) Eating nutritionally well-balanced meals.", "C) Growing into living and breathing human beings.", "D) Cultivating relationships with peers and teachers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say is one possible benefit问「作者说了什么好处」，是对作者\n明示信息的提取。\n【拆句】题干主干是What does the author say is one possible benefit，省略了关系代词的宾语\n从句结构「the author say (that) [what] is one possible benefit」；介词of短语of\nstudents attending school physically作benefit的定语，其中students是动名词\nattending的逻辑主语。结构无陷阱，注意one possible暗示文中可能列了多个好处，选项\n里只要有一个对得上即可。\n【定位】Physically attending school has hidden benefits: getting up every morning, interacting\nwith peers, and building relationships with teachers are essential skills to cultivate in\nyoung people.\n实地上学有隐性的好处：每天早起、与同伴互动、与老师建立关系，这些都是年轻人需要培\n养的关键能力。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nDeveloping the habit of getting up early虽然对应原文getting up every morning，\n但原文这只是三项并列好处之一，且作者强调的核心是skills to cultivate，而getting\nup属于日常生活动作；将「早起」提炼为「养成早起的习惯」是把动作误读成了一种习\n惯养成训练，与原文核心语义有偏差。\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文末尾They are now places that provide meals提到学校提供餐食，但作者并未说\n「营养均衡的餐食」是attending school physically的benefit；nutritionally well-\nbalanced属于无中生有的修饰语，且provide meals被原文归入「学校的社会职能」而\n非「实地上学的好处」——主体不同。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文a living, breathing human being teaches and interacts with students中living,\nbreathing是修饰teacher的形容词，强调「活生生的」教师；选项Growing into living\nand breathing human beings把「形容老师的状态词」偷换成「学生成长的目标」，\n且growing into是「成长为」的方向性动词，原文完全没有这层意思。\nD) ✓ 正确\ninteracting with peers, and building relationships with teachers ↔\nCultivating relationships with peers and teachers\n选项Cultivating relationships with peers and teachers精准合并了预判中的第二、三\n项内容，且与原文cultivate呼应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author think of the latest technology in education?", "options": ["A) It may have potential disadvantages.", "B) It may render many teachers jobless.", "C) It may add to students' financial burden.", "D) It may revolutionize classroom teaching"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度题）\n【判型】What does the author think of直问作者对某事物的看法，是典型态度类信号。\n【拆句】题干主干是What does the author think of the latest technology in education，think\nof是固定搭配「对……怎么看」；the latest是形容词最高级作定语。结构平铺直叙，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Those policy-makers are often fascinated by the latest technology in education and its\npotential to \"transform\" education overnight. But online education does not allow a teacher to\nkeep a struggling student after class and offer help.\n那些政策制定者常常被教育领域最新技术及其「一夜变革」教育的潜力所迷住。但在线教育\n不允许教师把学习吃力的学生留下来课后辅导。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nonline education does not allow a teacher to keep a struggling student after class and offer help\n↔ It may have potential disadvantages\nDistance education will never match the personal teaching in a traditional classroom ↔\nIt may have potential disadvantages\n预判得出作者对新技术持质疑或保留态度，It may have potential disadvantages恰好\n用「可能有不足」概括了这种保留立场，与原文极性一致。\nB) 无中生有\n原文未提及新技术会「让教师失业」；作者讨论的是技术替代教师在功能上的局限，不\n是技术造成教师失业的社会后果。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文谈的是节约预算者的成本动机resulting in greater savings，从未提及学生的财务\n负担会因此增加；把「学校省钱」误读为「学生多花钱」，方向完全相反。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt may revolutionize classroom teaching即「可能彻底变革课堂教学」是政策制定者\n的看法，不是作者的；原文用\"transform\"加引号、用But转折、用Distance education\nwill never match the personal teaching in a traditional classroom反驳，明确否定\n了这种乐观看法。考生若把fascinated误读成作者本人也「被迷住」，就会反选此项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say teachers are expected to do?", "options": ["A) Enhance students' leadership capacity.", "B) Elevate students to managerial positions.", "C) Enable students to adapt to the changes in life.", "D) Prepare students to be competitive in the future."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say teachers are expected to do?问「教师被期望做什么」，是\n对原文明示信息的客观提取。\n【拆句】题干主干是What does the author say teachers are expected to do，author say后省略\n了that引导的宾语从句；从句主干是「teachers are expected to do (what)」，be\nexpected to do是固定结构「被期望做」。结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Yet teachers are expected to prepare them to be responsible citizens, cultivate their social\nskills, encourage successful time management, and enhance their capacity to flourish in an\nincreasingly harsh labor market.\n然而，教师却被期望让他们成为有责任感的公民，培养他们的社交能力，鼓励他们成功管理\n时间，并增强他们在日益严酷的劳动力市场上立足发展的能力。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文四项期望中没有提到培养「领导力」（leadership capacity）；社交能力不等于领\n导力，二者不可等同。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文没有任何「把学生提升到管理岗位」的内容；该项利用原文management一词\n（time management）做望文生义的偷换——time management是「时间管理」，\nmanagerial positions是「管理职位」，二者只是共享management词根，语义完全\n不同。\nC) 无中生有\n原文四项期望中没有「让学生适应生活变化」（adapt to the changes in life）这一\n项；adapt to changes是常见教育鸡汤，但原文未提及。\nD) ✓ 正确\nenhance their capacity to flourish in an increasingly harsh labor market ↔\nPrepare students to be competitive in the future\n预判得出最后一项enhance their capacity to flourish in an increasingly harsh labor\nmarket，选项Prepare students to be competitive in the future恰是此项的抽象\n提炼。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "Why couldn't technology replace a good teacher?", "options": ["A) It lacks humanity.", "B) It is still immature.", "C) It cannot track students' growth.", "D) It cannot cater to personal needs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why couldn't technology replace a good teacher?问「为什么不能」，是对原文中给出\n的明确原因的提取；末段P4给出了直接答案句，不需推断。\n【拆句】题干主干是Why couldn't technology replace a good teacher?，否定情态动词couldn't\n与why连用，表达「为何不能」，注意这不是疑问句的常规反问，而是给定「不能取代」的\n前提下追问原因。结构无陷阱，重点抓couldn't的否定意味。\n【定位】The power of the classroom is rooted in the humanity of the people gathered in the same\nplace, at the same time.\n课堂的力量根植于聚集在同一地点、同一时间的人们身上的人性。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThe power of the classroom is rooted in the humanity of the people gathered in the same place,\nat the same time\n↔ It lacks humanity\nTechnology, no matter how advanced, should simply be a tool of a good teacher ↔\nIt lacks humanity\n预判得出原因为「技术缺乏人性」，选项It lacks humanity使用与原文同一个抽象名词\nhumanity，是同根词的正反极性替换，匹配度最高。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文未提及技术「尚不成熟」（still immature）；作者用no matter how advanced\n反而承认了技术可以非常先进，否定的是技术的本质属性（非人性），不是技术的发展\n阶段。\nC) 无中生有\n原文没有任何关于「技术无法追踪学生成长」的表述；track students' growth这一说法\n是作者批评教育技术时未涉及的另一个方向。\nD) 以偏概全\nIt cannot cater to personal needs即「不能满足个性化需求」听起来很对，且与第三\n段online education does not allow a teacher to keep a struggling student after\nclass and offer help有局部对应，但题目55题问的是技术为何不能取代好教师，根本原\n因应回末段P4的humanity，而不是第三段的某个具体功能局限；选It cannot cater to\npersonal needs是把局部理由当作根本理由，犯以偏概全的错误。这是本题最有迷惑性\n的干扰项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2022-06-1"});
